parallel.prompt:
Ticket:

${ticket}

Working brief (its acceptance criteria are the bar):

${brief}

Answers from the person:

${answers}

The engineer's note:

${change}

Review the committed change in your working directory, ${worktrees.repo.path} (`git log`, `git show`). Does it meet each acceptance criterion? Is it the right shape for this repository? Mark every finding blocking, should-fix or nice-to-have, with the file and line. Do not demand information the ticket does not give when the brief made a reasonable assumption, and do not invent nits.

Ticket:

${ticket}

Working brief (its acceptance criteria are the bar):

${brief}

The engineer's note:

${change}

Exercise the committed change in your working directory, ${worktrees.repo.path} the way its user would — reading the files is not enough. Run the tests or the build if there are any. For a web page: serve the directory on a free local port (for example `python3 -m http.server`), fetch the page and every asset it references (each must return 200), then load it in a headless browser (Chrome or Chromium, e.g. `--headless --screenshot=<file> --window-size=1280,2000 <url>`) and Read the screenshot to see what a visitor sees. To serve a page, start the server so you can stop exactly it: `python3 -m http.server <port> >/dev/null 2>&1 & SERVER_PID=$!` and later `kill $SERVER_PID`. Never stop processes by port (`lsof -ti:<port> | xargs kill` and the like) — that also matches your own process and ends your session. Stop any server you started.

Report PASS or FAIL for each acceptance criterion with the evidence (command, status codes, what the screenshot shows), then an overall PASS or FAIL.

